Inspect Kubernetes Resources
Objective
This topic teaches how to:
•	Query Kubernetes resources
•	Format command output
•	Filter specific attributes
In Kubernetes and OpenShift, everything is managed using API resource objects.
________________________________________
1. Kubernetes Resources
In Kubernetes, resources represent objects inside the cluster.
Examples:
•	Pods
•	Services
•	Deployments
•	ConfigMaps
•	Nodes
These resources define the desired state of the system.
Example:
If you create a deployment with 3 pods, Kubernetes ensures 3 pods are always running.
________________________________________
2. View Available Resources
Command:
oc api-resources
Example output:
NAME            SHORTNAMES   APIVERSION   NAMESPACED   KIND
pods            po           v1           true         Pod
services        svc          v1           true         Service
deployments     deploy       apps/v1      true         Deployment
replicasets     rs           apps/v1      true         ReplicaSet
________________________________________
Meaning of Columns
Column	Meaning
NAME	resource name
SHORTNAMES	shorter command alias
APIVERSION	API group and version
NAMESPACED	whether resource belongs to namespace
KIND	object schema type
________________________________________
Example of Shortname
Instead of:
oc get configmaps
you can use:
oc get cm
________________________________________
3. API Groups and Versions
API Version format:
<API-GROUP>/<VERSION>
Example:
apps/v1
Meaning:
Part	Meaning
apps	API group
v1	version
Core resources may appear as:
v1
(no API group).
________________________________________
4. Namespaced Resources
Some resources exist inside namespaces.
Example:
•	pods
•	services
•	deployments
Namespaces help separate applications.
In OpenShift:
Namespace = Project
There is 1-to-1 mapping.
________________________________________
5. Filtering api-resources
Example command:
oc api-resources --namespaced=true --api-group apps --sort-by name
Meaning:
Option	Purpose
--namespaced=true	show namespace resources
--api-group apps	show resources in apps group
--sort-by name	sort output
Example output:
daemonsets
deployments
replicasets
statefulsets
________________________________________
6. Understanding Resource Fields
Command:
oc explain pod
Output explains the structure of a resource.
Example fields:
apiVersion
kind
metadata
spec
status
________________________________________
Main Fields of Every Resource
Field	Purpose
apiVersion	schema version
kind	resource type
metadata	identification data
spec	desired configuration
status	current runtime state
________________________________________
7. spec vs status
These two fields are very important in Kubernetes architecture.
spec
Defines the desired state.
Example:
3 replicas required
status
Defines the current state.
Example:
2 replicas running
Kubernetes controller will create 1 more pod to match spec.
________________________________________
8. Viewing Specific Fields
Command:
oc explain pod.spec
Shows details for:
pod specification
Example fields inside spec:
•	containers
•	volumes
•	restartPolicy
•	nodeSelector
________________________________________
9. Major Kubernetes Resource Types
1. Pods
pod
•	smallest deployable unit
•	contains containers
•	shares:
o	IP address
o	storage
________________________________________
2. Services
svc
Provide network access to pods.
Example:
Client → Service → Pods
Service load balances traffic.
________________________________________
3. ReplicaSets
rs
Ensure a fixed number of pod replicas.
Example:
3 pods always running
________________________________________
4. Persistent Volumes
pv
Provide storage for pods.
Example:
database storage
________________________________________
5. Persistent Volume Claims
pvc
A request for storage.
Pods use PVC to connect with PV.
________________________________________
6. ConfigMaps
cm
Store configuration data.
Example:
DB_HOST
PORT
ENVIRONMENT
________________________________________
7. Secrets
Store sensitive data.
Examples:
•	passwords
•	API keys
•	certificates
Secrets are encoded (not encrypted).
________________________________________
8. Deployments
deploy
Manage pods and replicas.
Features:
•	scaling
•	rolling updates
•	rollback
________________________________________
10. OpenShift Specific Resources
OpenShift adds extra resources.
________________________________________
Build
build
Process that converts:
source code → container image
________________________________________
BuildConfig
bc
Defines:
•	build source
•	build strategy
•	output image
________________________________________
Routes
Routes expose applications outside the cluster.
Example:
https://myapp.apps.cluster.com
Similar to Ingress in Kubernetes.
________________________________________
11. Kubernetes Object Structure (YAML)
Example Pod YAML:
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: wildfly
  namespace: my_app
  labels:
    name: wildfly
spec:
  containers:
    - image: quay.io/example/todojee:v1
      name: wildfly
      ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
________________________________________
Important YAML Fields
apiVersion
apiVersion: v1
Defines API schema version.
________________________________________
kind
kind: Pod
Defines resource type.
________________________________________
metadata
Contains identification info:
metadata:
  name
  namespace
  labels
________________________________________
spec
Defines desired configuration.
Example:
containers
ports
volumes
environment variables
________________________________________
status
Generated automatically by Kubernetes.
Contains:
•	pod status
•	IP address
•	readiness
•	runtime information
________________________________________
12. Labels
Labels are key-value pairs used to organize resources.
Example:
labels:
  app: example
  group: developers
________________________________________
Use Labels to Filter Resources
Command:
oc get pods --selector group=developers
Output:
example-pod
Only pods with that label are shown.
________________________________________
13. Viewing Resource Output
Default command:
oc get pods
Example output:
NAME     READY   STATUS   RESTARTS   AGE
mypod    1/1     Running  0          2d
________________________________________
Wide Output
Command:
oc get pods -o wide
Extra information:
IP
NODE
________________________________________
14. Describe Command
Command:
oc describe pod mypod
Shows detailed information:
•	events
•	container details
•	errors
•	resource limits
Important for troubleshooting.
⚠ Not recommended for scripting.
Because output format may change.
________________________________________
15. YAML Output
Command:
oc get pods -o yaml
Output format:
YAML
Used for:
•	configuration
•	scripting
•	automation
Example field:
status.podIP
________________________________________
16. Using yq with YAML
Example:
oc get pods -o yaml | yq '.items[0].status.podIP'
Output:
10.8.0.60
Meaning:
•	get first pod
•	display its IP
________________________________________
17. JSON Output
Command:
oc get pods -o json
Example output:
{
 "apiVersion": "v1",
 "items": [...]
}
________________________________________
18. Using jq with JSON
Example:
oc get pods -o json | jq '.items[0].status.podIP'
Output:
"10.8.0.60"
________________________________________
19. Custom Columns Output
Command:
oc get pods \
-o custom-columns=PodName:".metadata.name",\
IP:"status.podIP"
Output:
PodName    IP
mypod      10.8.0.60
Useful for structured output.
________________________________________
20. JSONPath Queries
JSONPath extracts fields from JSON.
Example:
oc get pods \
-o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}'
Output:
mypod
________________________________________
Example with loop:
oc get pods -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{"\n"}{end}'
Lists all pod names.
________________________________________
21. Go Template Output
Command:
oc get pods \
-o go-template='{{range .items}}{{.metadata.name}}{{"\n"}}{{end}}'
Outputs:
mypod
mypod2
Used for advanced formatting.
